  "configuration_id": "config_042",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_046 (multimodal, multiprofile, scvi_n200, imagenet_vit_l, avgpool, weighted) achieved the best accuracy (0.7062), a massive jump over the best singleprofile result (config_006, 0.5375). This confirms multiprofile is the dominant design lever. However, we have only one multiprofile data point, using avgpool aggregation. The key uncertainty is whether a more expressive aggregation (transformer) can further improve over simple average pooling when combining multiple profiles. Question: config_042 (transformer, weighted) isolates the aggregation method by changing only avgpool→transformer relative to config_046, keeping RNA encoder, image encoder, profile setting, and fusion all identical. Why this is the best next experiment: Under a limited budget, understanding whether aggregation architecture matters is more informative than re-testing fusion (which showed negligible differences in singleprofile: config_006 vs config_021). If transformer aggregation yields a meaningful gain, it identifies a new optimization axis; if it does not, we can confidently conclude avgpool is sufficient and redirect budget toward other untested dimensions (e.g., image encoder choice or RNA encoder alternatives in the multiprofile setting)."
}

